From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] blur: keep textures per framebuffer; copy only the part on it

A background blur kept one set of textures. Each stage view is its own
framebuffer at its own scale, and the box the effect copied was the whole
actor in that view's pixels, so an actor painted on two views of a
different scale or size reallocated every texture on every paint. Under a
full-screen scrim over three monitors (scale 1, 1 and 1.25, 100-120 Hz) the
Shell's GPU memory swung between 4 and 12 GB in a 1.2 s cycle, about 9 GB a
second allocated and cleared, with the kernel's workers busy clearing pages.
The copy and both passes also covered the whole actor on every view, though
only the part inside the framebuffer is defined.

- One target per framebuffer (stage view, or NULL for an off-stage
  capture): its textures, pipelines and cache state. An actor blur keeps a
  single target. Targets unpainted for 5 s are dropped; at most 4 are kept.
- A background blur copies and blurs only the part of the actor inside the
  framebuffer, and draws it over that part of the actor; the brightness
  shader's corner mask takes the covered rectangle as its origin and extent.
  An actor with nothing inside the framebuffer draws no blur there.
- background_was_painted() compares the redraw clip with the part of the
  actor on that view. The clip of one view never contains an actor that
  reaches another monitor, so such an actor kept a stale blur and queued a
  repaint every frame on every view.
- shell_blur_effect_get_framebuffer_allocations() counts allocations for
  tests.

Headless, three virtual monitors at 1, 1 and 1.25 with the Share picker
open: 2 frames a second before, 56 after, with no texture allocations while
the picker is up.
---
